    bool "180"

config INPUT_IQS9151_ROTATE_270
    bool "270"
endchoice

config INPUT_IQS9151_RESOLUTION_X
    int "IQS9151 X resolution"
    range 0 4095
    default 2457
    help
      X Resolution (0x11E6), written to the IC at init.
      Limited to 0-4095 to match the practical 12-bit trackpad
      coordinate scale used by the device configuration.

      **This is not the cursor speed knob.** Measured on real hardware, across
      three flashes and both halves: moving X from 1300 to 1974 changed the
      pointer not at all. The Relative X/Y the cursor is built from does not
      come out of these registers. Use INPUT_IQS9151_CURSOR_GAIN_X_X10 for
      pointer speed.

      What it does set is the range of the *absolute* finger coordinates, and
      those are what every gesture decision in this driver compares -- which
      axis a scroll is on, whether a swipe beat the other direction, whether
      fingers spreading beat the pair travelling. So set the two in proportion
      to the pad's physical sides and those comparisons mean angles; leave them
      mismatched and all of them lean toward the denser axis, with no threshold
      adjustment able to straighten it out.

      Start from the sensor's own value for the shorter side and scale the
      other by the ratio of the sides.

config INPUT_IQS9151_RESOLUTION_Y
    int "IQS9151 Y resolution"
    range 0 4095
    default 3072
    help
      Y Resolution (0x11E8), written to the IC at init.
      Limited to 0-4095 to match the practical 12-bit trackpad
      coordinate scale used by the device configuration.

      See INPUT_IQS9151_RESOLUTION_X: the two only mean anything as a pair.
      With INPUT_IQS9151_RUNTIME_SETTINGS both become the *defaults* for a
      setting the owner can move from the app, which is the practical way to
      find the pair -- the board drawing gives the copper outline, not the
      electrode array, and a ratio derived from the wrong one is wrong by
      however much those two differ.

config INPUT_IQS9151_CURSOR_GAIN_X_X10
    int "IQS9151 cursor gain, sensor X axis (tenths)"
    range 1 200
    default 10
    help
      Multiplier applied to the relative movement the device reports on its X
      axis, in tenths: 10 is unchanged, 16 is 1.6x.

      This exists because the resolution registers do not do it. Those set the
      range of the *absolute* finger coordinates, which is what the gesture
      arbitration compares -- so they stay the right lever for which axis a
      scroll is on -- but the Relative X/Y the cursor is built from does not
      come out of them. Changing X resolution and measuring no change in cursor
      speed is the expected result, not a broken write.

      A pad longer one way than the other needs its long axis multiplied to
      move the pointer as far for the same finger travel. On a keyboard it
      usually wants a little more than the ratio of the sides, because the long
      side cannot be swept end to end in one stroke the way the short one can:
      matching millimetres still leaves that direction feeling reluctant.

      Fractional gains work because the tenths are carried between reports.
      Without that carry, a 1.6x gain on the one- and two-count reports a
      trackpad actually sends rounds back to 1x and does nothing.

config INPUT_IQS9151_CURSOR_GAIN_Y_X10
    int "IQS9151 cursor gain, sensor Y axis (tenths)"
    range 1 200
    default 10
    help
      The same for the sensor's Y axis. Note the listener's XY_SWAP: on a pad
      mounted like ErgoTrack's, the sensor's X is the screen's vertical and its
      Y is the screen's horizontal.

      Raising one axis and lowering the other keeps the overall pointer speed
      roughly where it was while changing the balance between them.

config INPUT_IQS9151_RECOVERY_STACK_SIZE
    int "IQS9151 reset-recovery thread stack size"
    default 1536
    help
      Stack for the thread that rewrites the device's configuration after the
      device reports it has reset itself (SHOW_RESET).

      It exists so that rebuild does not run on the system work queue. The
      rebuild is the same sequence init performs -- acknowledge the reset,
      write the settings blob in chunks, apply the overrides, re-tune ATI --
      which is seconds of I2C with sleeps in it. ZMK's watchdog watches the
      system work queue by feeding from a work item on it, so occupying that
      queue for seconds reboots the keyboard with nothing in the log to explain
      it. A trackpad having a bad moment should not be able to do that.

config INPUT_IQS9151_RECOVERY_THREAD_PRIORITY
    int "IQS9151 reset-recovery thread priority"
    default 10
    help
      Cooperative-range priorities are a bad idea here: the rebuild sleeps and
      waits on a busy device, and must never hold the CPU against the input or
      Bluetooth work that keeps the keyboard usable. A low preemptible priority
      is right -- the pad is already not working while this runs, so finishing
      quickly matters less than not getting in the way.

config INPUT_IQS9151_ATI_TARGETCOUNT
    int "IQS9151 ATI target count"
    range 0 1000
    default 250
    help
      Trackpad ATI Target (0x119A).
      Limited to 0-1000 to avoid unstable behavior.
      Currently not applied by the driver.

