    default y
    help
      Enable driver-side two-finger pinch detection.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_PINCH_START_DISTANCE
    int "IQS9151 two-finger pinch start threshold"
    range 1 2000
    default 100
    help
      Distance-delta threshold to start 2F pinch mode (driver-side).

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_PINCH_DOMINANCE_X10
    int "IQS9151 how decisively a pinch must beat a scroll (tenths)"
    range 10 100
    default 10
    help
      How much larger the change in the distance between the two fingers must
      be than the distance their centroid travelled, before two fingers are
      read as a pinch rather than a scroll. In tenths: 10 is "merely larger",
      20 is "twice as large".

      Both numbers come from the same pair of fingers. A careful scroll moves
      the centroid and leaves the spacing alone, so 10 is enough. A hurried one
      is not so tidy -- fingers splay a little as they travel -- and on a small
      pad that is enough to land an intended scroll on pinch. Raising this asks
      that the spreading be the dominant thing happening, without making a
      deliberate pinch need a wider spread the way raising the start threshold
      would.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_PINCH_WHEEL_GAIN_X10
    int "IQS9151 two-finger pinch wheel gain (x10)"
    range 1 100
    default 40
    help
      Gain for 2F pinch REL_WHEEL output inside the driver.
      10 = 1.0x, 20 = 2.0x, 5 = 0.5x.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_PINCH_WHEEL_HORIZONTAL
    bool "IQS9151 report pinch on the horizontal wheel"
    default n
    help
      Report the 2F pinch wheel as REL_HWHEEL instead of REL_WHEEL. Enable
      this when the listener corrects a rotated pad with
      zip_scroll_transform INPUT_TRANSFORM_XY_SWAP: that swap applies to
      every wheel event, so pinch would otherwise arrive at the host as a
      horizontal scroll and never zoom.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_PINCH_INVERT
    bool "IQS9151 invert the two-finger pinch direction"
    default n
    help
      Turn the pinch wheel the other way, so spreading the fingers zooms out
      and closing them zooms in.

      Which direction is right is the host's convention rather than anything
      about the pad, so there is no value that is correct everywhere. With
      INPUT_IQS9151_RUNTIME_SETTINGS this is only the default and the owner
      can change it from the app; without it, it is the answer.

config INPUT_IQS9151_RUNTIME_SETTINGS
    bool "IQS9151 settings the owner can change from the app"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_CUSTOM_SETTINGS
    help
      Publish the trackpad decisions that are the user's rather than the
      board's -- whether one-pad pinch is offered, and which way it zooms --
      as custom settings, so they can be changed from a configurator instead
      of by rebuilding and reflashing.

      The Kconfig switches above become the defaults. A keyboard nobody has
      touched behaves exactly as its .conf says.

      On a split, enable this on BOTH halves: each half classifies gestures on
      its own sensor and reads its own copy of the setting. The write reaches
      both only if the client asks for every side
      (ZMK_CUSTOM_SETTING_SOURCE_ALL), which needs
      ZMK_CUSTOM_SETTINGS_SPLIT_RPC_RELAY on both halves too.

config INPUT_IQS9151_RUNTIME_SETTINGS_STUDIO_RPC
    bool "IQS9151 expose the runtime settings to Studio"
    default y
    depends on INPUT_IQS9151_RUNTIME_SETTINGS && ZMK_STUDIO
    select ZMK_CUSTOM_SETTINGS_STUDIO_RPC
    help
      Register the trackpad as a Studio custom subsystem. There is no RPC of
      its own, but a setting whose subsystem id matches no registered
      subsystem is dropped from ListSettings, so without this the settings
      exist on the keyboard and the app cannot see them.

      Only the central half needs this.

