config INPUT_IQS9151_3F_SWIPE_THRESHOLD
    int "IQS9151 three-finger swipe threshold (sensor X axis)"
    range 0 1000
    default 200
    help
      How far three fingers must travel along the sensor's X axis before the
      movement counts as a swipe.

config INPUT_IQS9151_3F_SWIPE_THRESHOLD_Y
    int "IQS9151 three-finger swipe threshold (sensor Y axis)"
    range 0 1000
    default INPUT_IQS9151_3F_SWIPE_THRESHOLD
    help
      The same for the sensor's Y axis. Defaults to the X threshold, so a board
      with nothing to say about this keeps the single number it always had.

      Worth lowering on a pad that is much longer one way than the other. Three
      fingers lie across the short side and take up most of it, so there is very
      little room left to travel that way before one of them runs off the
      sensor -- while the long side has room to spare. One threshold for both
      makes the cramped direction the hard one no matter how comfortable the
      other feels.

config INPUT_IQS9151_SWIPE_INVERT_X
    bool "IQS9151 invert the swipe X axis"
    default n
    help
      Swap which way a swipe along the sensor's X axis counts, so a gesture
      that reported BTN_3 reports BTN_4 and the other way round.

      A swipe is classified in the driver, on the sensor's own coordinates,
      because it is one decision about a whole gesture rather than a stream of
      deltas. The listener's XY_SWAP / X_INVERT / Y_INVERT chain -- which is
      what normally squares a rotated pad with the screen -- therefore never
      sees it, and a pad mounted the other way round moves the cursor
      correctly while swiping backwards. This is that correction.

config INPUT_IQS9151_SWIPE_INVERT_Y
    bool "IQS9151 invert the swipe Y axis"
    default n
    help
      The same for the sensor's Y axis: the three-finger pair BTN_5/BTN_6, and
      the two-finger horizontal swipe below.

      Setting both is the 180-degree case: the mirror-image half of a split,
      where the same sensor part is fitted upside down and every swipe comes
      out backwards on both axes.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_SWIPE_ENABLE
    bool "IQS9151 two-finger horizontal swipe"
    default n
    help
      Report a decisive sideways movement of two fingers as a single key tap,
      so the keymap can put browser back/forward -- or anything else -- on it.
      The two directions report BTN_SIDE and BTN_EXTRA; which is which depends
      on how the pad is mounted, so try it rather than assuming.

      This costs two-finger horizontal *scroll*, and the trade is not
      avoidable: a sideways movement cannot both start a scroll immediately and
      be held back long enough to be recognised as a gesture. While a movement
      is decisively sideways, scroll is not allowed to start; one that turns
      out to be vertical after all scrolls as usual. Vertical scroll is
      untouched.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_SCROLL_AXIS_LOCK_X10
    int "IQS9151 keep a two-finger scroll on the axis it started on (tenths)"
    range 0 100
    default 0
    help
      How much one axis must lead the other, when a scroll starts, for the
      scroll to be held to that axis for the rest of the gesture. In tenths:
      12 means "1.2x the other", which is everything within roughly 40 degrees
      of straight. 0 disables it and both axes pass through as they always did.

      A trackpad in a keyboard is not always approached square-on. Reaching for
      it from the home row puts the hand at an angle, and then "straight up" is
      a diagonal as far as the pad is concerned -- so a scroll meant as vertical
      arrives as vertical-plus-a-bit-sideways and the page drifts while it
      scrolls. Picking the axis once, at the start, is what stops that.

      This compares the two axes' counts, which is only an angle if both axes
      report the same counts per millimetre. On a pad whose sides differ that is
      a resolution question -- see INPUT_IQS9151_RESOLUTION_X.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_SWIPE_THRESHOLD
    int "IQS9151 two-finger horizontal swipe threshold"
    range 1 1000
    default 200
    help
      How far sideways the two fingers must travel before the swipe is
      reported.

config INPUT_IQS9151_2F_SWIPE_DOMINANCE_X10
    int "IQS9151 how sideways a two-finger swipe must be (tenths)"
    range 10 100
    default 20
    help
      How much larger the sideways travel must be than the vertical travel for
      a two-finger movement to count as a horizontal swipe. In tenths: 20 means
      twice as far sideways as up or down.

      This is also what keeps a slightly crooked vertical scroll out of the
      swipe: below this ratio the movement is a scroll, whatever its length.

config INPUT_IQS9151_TEST
    bool "IQS9151 test hooks"
    depends on ZTEST
    default n
    help
      Expose IQS9151 internal test APIs and event hooks for ZTEST.

endif
